    "reader": "The model passes three of the four checks cleanly. (i) Zero mean: the residual mean is 0.00018 percentage points, with t = 0.02, so there is no bias left in the forecast. (ii) Constant variance: the split-half variance ratio is 1.005 (p = 0.96) and the squared residuals show no trend (p = 0.58), so the variance is homoskedastic in the unconditional sense. (iii) Residual autocorrelation: the ACF stays inside the Bartlett bands, and Ljung-Box does not reject white noise at 6, 12, 18 or 24 lags; at 36 lags the p-value falls to 0.042, which is the expected behaviour of a portmanteau test applied at a lag length far beyond the estimated orders (the statistic accumulates 36 sample autocorrelations, none of which is individually significant). (iv) Parsimony: the selected model beats both its over- and under-parameterised neighbours on AICc and BIC. Two qualifications must be reported. First, the residual distribution is leptokurtic (excess kurtosis 4.1) and the ARCH-LM test rejects no-ARCH at 12 lags, i.e. inflation volatility clusters around 1973-74 and 2008-09. The point forecasts are unaffected -- the conditional mean equations are unchanged -- but the Gaussian confidence intervals are too narrow in turbulent episodes. Second, dropping the two largest residuals (2008-11 and 1973-08) shifts the coefficients only slightly, so the estimates are not driven by outliers."
